        "type": "tekst",
        "titel": "4. Ideology: stratification’s staying power",
        "tekst": "Why do unequal societies persist? Because stratification is built on **[[ideology|Ideology]]**: **cultural beliefs that legitimate key interests and justify [[social stratification|Social stratification]]** (\"the rich are clever, the poor are lazy\").\n\n**Plato** defined justice as agreement about who should have what: every society teaches its members that its system is fair. **Marx** was more critical: capitalism presents inequality as \"a law of the marketplace\", protected by property and inheritance law; \"ideas as well as resources are controlled by a society's elite\". Both saw ideology not as a conspiracy but as cultural patterns built up over time.\n\n**Historical patterns:** **Aristotle** defended [[slavery|Slavery]] (some people deserve to be directed by their \"betters\"); medieval Europe saw the [[estate system|Estate system]] as **God's will** (\"The rich man in his castle, the poor man at his gate, He made them high and lowly and ordered their estate\"); industrial capitalism replaced birth with **individual achievement and merit**, so that poverty changed from an object of charity into a sign of **personal inadequacy**.\n\n**Ideology, gender and ethnicity**: ideas about \"a woman's place\" are losing their power, but caste-like expectations remain (most professional chefs are men, most home cooks women); [[apartheid|Apartheid]] lost its support as \"natural\".",
