        "type": "tekst",
        "titel": "5. Stratification as functional: the Davis-Moore thesis",
        "tekst": "**Davis and Moore (1945)**: [[social stratification|Social stratification]] has **beneficial consequences** for society; that is why it exists everywhere. The more **functionally important** a position (transplanting an organ versus changing spark plugs), the more **rewards** society attaches to it (income, prestige, power, leisure), so that talented people are motivated to do the most important work. Equal rewards would remove the incentive and reduce productivity.\n\n**[[Meritocracy]]**: a system of stratification based on **personal merit**: **equality of opportunity** with **inequality of rewards**, and extensive mobility. Caste societies only honour those who stay \"in their place\". Industrial societies keep some caste-like elements (family inheritance) because pure meritocracy would erode social structures such as kinship.\n\n**Critique of Davis-Moore:**\n1. It cannot explain why systems differ so much (harsh or flexible).\n2. It is hard to **measure functional importance**: nurses earn far less than surgeons; footballers and pop stars earn more in a night than teachers in a lifetime.\n3. It **exaggerates** the role of talent: families pass on wealth and power, and women, ethnic groups and disabled people face barriers.\n4. It **ignores conflict**: inequality can cause unrest and even revolution (Tumin, 1953).\n\n**Public Sociology: are the rich worth what they earn?** Care workers earn about £5 an hour, while celebrities and stars earn millions. The market defence: rare talents in demand. The critique: executives pay themselves even when firms fail, and many socially vital workers get little. \"What is lucrative may or may not be socially valuable.\"",
