        "type": "tekst",
        "titel": "6. Marx: stratification and conflict",
        "tekst": "**Conflict analysis**: stratification benefits **some at the expense of others**. **Marx** (with Engels, *The Communist Manifesto*, 1848): two classes defined by their relation to the **means of production**: those who **own** productive property (the **bourgeoisie**, capitalists) and those who **labour** for others (the **proletariat**). Exploitation and misery would make workers **pauperised, polarised and class conscious**, leading to revolution. The elite reproduces itself through **family, inheritance law and exclusive schools**.\n\n**Critique**: Marx separated reward from performance (\"from each according to ability; to each according to need\"), which critics link to the **low productivity** of Soviet-type economies; defenders argue humans are social, not only selfish. And the revolution did not come in advanced capitalist societies.\n\n**Why no Marxist revolution? Dahrendorf (1959), four reasons:**\n1. **Fragmentation of the capitalist class**: many stockholders and a managerial class instead of single owning families.\n2. **White-collar work and a rising standard of living**: most workers now do mental work; living standards rose fourfold in the twentieth century.\n3. **More extensive worker organisation**: unions and institutionalised, peaceful negotiation.\n4. **More extensive legal protections**: workers' rights, courts, social security, health and safety.\nThe book adds: a media-saturated society in which we may \"amuse ourselves to death\" (Postman) and lose our critical edge.\n\n**The critical case: five replies from neo-Marxists:**\n1. **Wealth remains highly concentrated** and inequality has increased (about half of private corporate stock in Europe owned by 1%).\n2. **Global capitalism**: production moved to low-income countries: \"the sweatshops of the world\".\n3. **Work remains degrading**: routine white-collar work, temporary and low-paid jobs.\n4. **Labour activity has been weakened**: union decline.\n5. **The law still favours the rich**.",
