        "type": "tekst",
        "titel": "7. Weber: class, status and power",
        "tekst": "**Max Weber** agreed that stratification causes conflict but found Marx's two classes **too simple**. Stratification has **three distinct dimensions**:\n1. **Class**: economic position, on a continuum from high to low.\n2. **Status**: social **prestige**.\n3. **Power**.\n\nBecause industrial societies have **low [[status consistency|Status consistency]]** (a bureaucrat with power but little wealth or prestige), there are many overlapping categories, all pursuing their own interests; conflict is complex. **[[Socioeconomic status (SES)]]**: a composite ranking based on several dimensions of inequality.\n\n**Inequality in history**: agrarian societies emphasise **status** (honour, purity); industrial capitalism makes **class** (economic differences) crucial; the growth of the bureaucratic state brings **power** to the fore, also in socialist societies, whose elites are officials rather than rich people. Weber therefore doubted that abolishing capitalism would end stratification: a socialist revolution might **increase** inequality by concentrating power in a political elite, as the uprisings in Eastern Europe seemed to confirm.\n\n**Critique**: influential, especially in Europe, but Marxists point out that **economic polarisation** grew again from the 1980s, so the focus returned to classes in conflict.",
