        "type": "tekst",
        "titel": "8. Technology, the Kuznets curve and the Bell Curve debate",
        "tekst": "**Gerhard and Jean Lenski's socio-cultural evolution** links technology to stratification:\n- **Hunting and gathering**: no surplus, everything shared; stratification only by age and sex.\n- **Horticultural, pastoral and agrarian**: surplus produces more inequality; in agrarian societies the gap between nobility and serfs is as large as ever in history.\n- **Industrial**: inequality **decreases** somewhat: individual talent matters, living standards and literacy rise, people demand a political voice, blue-collar work becomes white-collar, and male dominance weakens. That is why Marxist revolutions happened in **agrarian** societies (Russia 1917, Cuba 1959, Nicaragua 1979), not industrial ones.\n\n**The [[Kuznets curve]]** (Simon Kuznets, 1966): technological progress first **sharply increases** and then **moderates** [[social stratification|Social stratification]] (an inverted U). Mature industrial societies (the EU, Australia, the US) have less income inequality than the less industrialised countries of Latin America, Africa and Asia; but politics matters too, and the curve does not guarantee that inequality will keep falling.\n\n**Public Sociology: [[the Bell Curve|The Bell Curve]] debate** (Herrnstein and Murray, 1994): claimed that a \"general intelligence\" exists, is 60-70% genetic, and that a **\"cognitive elite\"** now dominates while the poor have lower intelligence, so social programmes help little. **Critics**: intelligence cannot be separated from socialisation (rich children have all the advantages), most researchers put heritability at no more than **25-40%**, and the book is a rehash of **social Darwinism**.",
